.PP
The current release implements both tiers. The vm tier is Kata Containers
via Docker, and requires /dev/kvm. Podman is not offered for this tier: its
VM path is libkrun, whose crun handler implements no exec callback, so
nothing can enter a box it created. Where hardware or runtime is missing, a
project
declaring
.B min_isolation = \[dq]vm\[dq]
exits 69 naming what would fix it rather than silently substituting the
weaker tier. The Layer 3 filtered share (mask_mode = \[dq]filter\[dq])
ships in this release: under vm, \[dq]auto\[dq] resolves to
\[dq]filter\[dq] where the seam is deliverable (/dev/fuse present, plus
either agentbox running as root or the setuid fusermount3 helper with
user_allow_other enabled in /etc/fuse.conf) and falls back to
\[dq]view\[dq] with a warning on stderr otherwise; an explicit
\[dq]filter\[dq] that cannot be delivered is an error, never a downgrade.
The share daemon needs no privilege of its own on the helper route: the
masks hold because the daemon sits outside the guest, not because it is
root.
.SH UNIVERSAL REFUSALS
Under both tiers, agentbox refuses to construct a box with host networking,
a host PID namespace, a mounted host container-runtime socket, or an
unconfined seccomp profile \(em any one of these makes the rest decorative,
and a mounted runtime socket in particular is equivalent to handing the
agent root on the host.
.PP
Boxes cannot reach one another, over the network or through shared state:
each box gets its own network, its own persistent home, and its own mask
view. Two boxes means two workspace roots, so they do not share a working
tree either.
.SH MASKING
Masking constructs the masked view on the host side of the filesystem
boundary and presents that to the guest, so enforcement does not depend on
the guest's privilege level. Under view mode, matched files are covered
read-only (reads return EOF, writes fail) and matched directories appear
empty, with writes landing in memory and never reaching the host. Under
filter mode (vm tier), the workspace is served by agentbox's own share
daemon evaluating the patterns at every lookup: a masked path answers
ENOENT and is omitted from directory listings, and creating, linking, or
renaming onto a masked name fails EPERM. Renaming a directory that hides a
masked entry fails EBUSY, so an anchored pattern cannot be dodged by moving
an ancestor out from under it \(em the same move view mode blocks because
the masked path is a live mountpoint. The daemon compiles its patterns
from contents frozen host-side at box start, so editing
.B .agentignore
inside the box cannot unmask anything.
.PP
Documented limitations, scoped to the mode they apply to:
.IP 1. 4
.B Mounts are fixed at creation (view mode only).
A path that does not exist when the box is created cannot be masked; a
.B .env
the agent writes mid-session is readable. Under filter mode this does not
apply: the filter is evaluated live, and a matching file is masked the
moment it appears.
.IP 2. 4
.B A masked directory is a small writable ramdisk, not a hole (view mode only).
Masking a directory the toolchain builds into \(em
.BR node_modules ", " obj/ ", " .venv/
\(em fails the build once it exceeds
.BR masking.tmpfs_size .
Under filter mode there is no tmpfs; a masked directory simply does not
exist in the guest.
.IP 3. 4
.B .git/ should not be masked.
It costs diff, commit, and history for almost no gain.
It is deliberately absent from the scaffolded patterns.
.IP 4. 4
.B Masking is not a secrets manager.
For credentials that need not sit in the tree at all, prefer
.B [variables.passthrough]
or keeping the secret host-side. Masking exists for files an application
genuinely needs on disk to run \(em a dev TLS key, a seeded database \(em
where deleting them to run an agent would cost the ability to debug at all.
.SH NETWORK POLICY
In the default
.B proxy
mode the guest has no route to any external address; all egress goes
through a policy proxy evaluating a default-deny allowlist, so a tool that
ignores
.B HTTPS_PROXY
fails closed rather than escaping. TLS is not intercepted: the proxy
matches on the CONNECT target, leaving certificate validation end-to-end.
Every request, including denials, is logged.
.B deny
entries are evaluated first and win. An HTTP CONNECT proxy cannot carry
SSH: git operations must use HTTPS remotes in proxy mode.
.PP
Both tiers use the same topology, so an allowlist means one thing: a per-box
internal bridge with no route off it, and a squid sidecar straddling that
bridge and a second, routable network. The policy engine, its generated
configuration and its hardening are shared code between the tiers.
.PP
The vm tier differs only in how the box addresses that sidecar, and for one
reason. A Kata guest can reach a sibling container over an ordinary bridge,
but it cannot reach the engine's embedded DNS resolver: the engine publishes
it at 127.0.0.11 inside the network namespace, and a VM guest's loopback is
its own. Container names therefore do not resolve, so the vm tier addresses
its sidecar by a pinned IP. In
.B proxy
mode the guest needs no resolver at all \(em the proxy resolves on its
behalf, which also denies the box a DNS channel of its own. Only
.B open
mode needs one, and there agentbox bind-mounts a generated
.B /etc/resolv.conf
carrying the host's real upstream nameservers.
.SH GUEST ROOT
Under the container tier, guest root is denied by construction: the image
has no sudo, setuid bits are stripped, and the backend refuses to construct
a container-tier box that asks for guest root. The reasoning: a guest process with CAP_SYS_ADMIN could
unmount a mask and read through it, converting every mask from a boundary
into a suggestion \(em silently, with no error and no log line. The vm tier
is where the frequent agent request for root can be granted, because mask
enforcement lives outside the guest.
.SH A COMMITTED CONFIG OUTRANKS YOURS
.B agentbox.toml
is committed, so cloning a repository applies configuration you did not
write, above your own user configuration.
.PP
agentbox does not execute anything host-side on its behalf: there are no
hooks and no host-path mounts in the schema, so the worst a workspace config
can do is ask for a weaker box. It is allowed to, and it cannot do so
quietly. Any workspace value that loosens a ranked security key relative to
a lower layer produces a warning on every invocation, naming the key, both
values, and the file responsible. Tightening is silent. See
.BR agentbox.toml (5)
for the ranked keys.
.SH NO SILENT DIVERGENCE
One configuration file drives two non-equivalent boundaries, so divergence
must be loud:
every invocation that starts a box prints the backend, tier, egress mode and
mask mode in force, and
.B status
prints the same;
.B version
names each unavailable backend and why;
.B min_isolation
is honored without downgrade;
backend-specific keys are scope-checked statically on every machine; a box
created with
.B \-\-force\-isolation
warns on every invocation and is flagged by
.BR status .
The failure mode defended against is concrete: a configuration verified on
a bare-metal workstation runs weaker inside a colleague's cloud VM while
every command still succeeds.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agentbox (1),
.BR agentbox.toml (5)
